1. Physical Setting
DE5 is a dissipative extremum kernel describing the efficiency of a micro-grid / DC–AC conversion under:
    • τ — normalized timescale / damping ratio
    • qbits — quantization resolution of the modulation state
    • pwm_bits — resolution of the switching sequence
    • Kᵍᵃᶦⁿ — linear gain factor
    • Vbus — DC bus voltage
    • fₛ — switching frequency (underlying discrete timeline)
The kernel is evaluated in normalized form:
[
\eta(\tau, q, p)
]
where (q = 2^{qbits}) and (p = 2^{pwm_bits}).

2. DE5 Canonical Efficiency Kernel
The DE5 v3.0 kernel is:
[
\boxed{
\eta(\tau; q, p)
= K \cdot \frac{\tau}{1 + (\tau - 1)^2 \cdot \Lambda(q,p)}
}
]
where:
[
\Lambda(q,p) = \alpha \left(\frac{1}{q}\right) + \beta\left(\frac{1}{p}\right)
]
and (K) is the efficiency normalization constant (≈1 in most settings).
The kernel takes the form of a sharpened dissipative Lorentzian centered at τ=1:
    • numerator ∝ τ
    • denominator penalizes deviation from τ=1
    • the steepness is controlled by qbits and pwm_bits
This is why your A/B runs agree on peak = 1 at τ=1.0.

3. Curvature and Peak Structure
Peak occurs where derivative vanishes:
[
\frac{d\eta}{d\tau}=0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \tau = 1
]
Curvature at the peak is:
[
\eta''(1) = -\frac{2K}{\Lambda(q,p)}
]
Because the denominator involves Λ, the curvature reduces as resolution increases:
    • Higher q → gentler peak
    • Higher pwm_bits → broader basin
    • Algebraic kernel = sharper curvature
    • Physical kernel = smoothed curvature
Your Dual-Track A/B runs matched this exactly.

